  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This preprint has already shown early downstream reuse, with two documented biomedical article mentions, and it later appeared in a peer-reviewed journal version. The current record is otherwise quiet: OpenAlex lists no citations yet, so the strongest evidence of impact is still its reuse and publication trajectory rather than citation accumulation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><i>Vibrio cholerae</i> serotype impacts pathogenicity addresses a long-standing question in cholera biology: whether the common O1 serotype switch between Ogawa and Inaba reflects immune-driven adaptation and whether that switch affects virulence. The abstract frames the work around the O1 serogroup\u2019s central role in cholera pandemics and the biological difference between serotypes, making the study immediately relevant to microbiology, infectivity, and outbreak dynamics.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of documented reach, the clearest signals are modest but real. The preprint has two recorded downstream biomedical mentions in Europe PMC, indicating that the work has already been taken up in the scholarly literature. It also has a later peer-reviewed journal version, showing that the preprint progressed into formal publication rather than remaining a stand-alone manuscript.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader attention record is still limited. OpenAlex reports zero citations so far, and the available event-data sources do not show recorded online mentions in the digest. That makes this an early-stage impact profile: the article has begun to appear in the literature, but the citation base has not yet built.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The stewardship and reuse signals reinforce that the work is being preserved and indexed across platforms. OpenAIRE identifies multiple open instances, including the preprint and the published article, which supports discoverability and access. The article is also explicitly linked to a dataset-citation workflow, suggesting that the record captures data reuse as well as article-level reuse, even though the present digest does not enumerate those datasets.</p>",
